WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.860
For eleven years he hosted one of the most
popular art shows on television -- transforming

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.209
a blank canvas to a finished painting in a
remarkably short, 30 minutes.

00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:14.420
He captivated audiences with his mesmerizing
voice and relaxed demeanor, and of course,

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.639
his signature perm.

00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:19.859
Nearly twenty-five years after his death,
the painter of “happy little trees” is

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:25.460
immortalized through internet memes, inspirational
quotes, a thriving business in his name, and

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.529
reruns of his show The Joy of Painting.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:32.800
His fame as a pop-culture icon shows no sign
of diminishing yet so little is known about

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:33.840
his life.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:41.899
Today on Biographics, we piece together the
world of Bob Ross -- beyond the canvas.

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:48.430
Personal Life

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:52.890
Bob Ross was fiercely private and never revealed
details of his personal life.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.570
He gave very few interviews -- claiming no
one bothered to ask him.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:01:00.440
Looking for answers is especially difficult
considering no official biography of his life

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.629
exists from primary sources.

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:06.260
The one documentary, “Bob Ross: The Happy
Painter” can only be obtained by pledging

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:08.162
money to PBS, or getting a hold of a DVD.

00:01:08.162 --> 00:01:14.020
The book, Happy Clouds, Happy Trees: The Bob
Ross Phenomenon, admitted that their “text

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:17.930
is… about an understanding we have of Bob
Ross and his life.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:22.750
If we had wanted to write an accurate biographical
book on Bob Ross, that goal would be difficult

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:23.980
to accomplish.”

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:28.829
Adding to the elusive Ross; he was said to
have very few friends and they, out of respect,

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:34.020
only ever conducted interviews for the aforementioned
documentary that is not readily or widely

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.020
available.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:38.610
Facts are hard to come by, and nearly impossible
to verify.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:42.740
Still, some specifics are known and so far,
remain undisputed.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.010
Here’s what we know:

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:50.060
Robert (Bob) Norman Ross was born on October
29, 1942 in Daytona Beach, Florida to Jack,

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:52.509
a builder and carpenter, and Ollie, a waitress.

00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:56.490
When Ross was just a year and a half old his
parents divorced and Ross moved to Orlando.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:01.930
Not much is known of Ross’ childhood in
Orlando -- save for two defining moments.

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:07.390
As a freshman in high school, Ross dropped
out to work as a carpenter alongside his father.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.739
Around the same time, Ross suffered an accident,
losing the tip of his left index finger on

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:12.849
a saw blade.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:16.519
Ross must have been self-conscious of his
deformed hand for the rest of his life.

00:02:16.519 --> 00:02:20.480
On his hit television show The Joy of Painting,
Ross carefully disguised it -- holding his

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.180
paint palette so it remained shielded from
view most of the time.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.280
Ross was known to be an animal lover his entire
life.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:29.870
As a youngster, this may have been passed
on from his mother.

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:34.709
As a teenager, Ross allegedly kept exotic
pets, including armadillos and alligators.

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:39.469
Later on in life, his pet gray squirrel sometimes
made an appearance on his television show.

00:02:39.469 --> 00:02:43.450
Ross once said, “If we’re going to have
animals around we all have to be concerned

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:45.300
about them and take care of them.”

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.299
Bob Ross married twice during his life and
had three sons.

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:52.510
He married his first wife Lynda Brown early
on in his military career and they divorced

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:53.749
in 1981.

00:02:53.749 --> 00:02:56.480
The couple had two sons, Bob Jr. and Steven.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.599
Steven would follow in his father’s footsteps
and become a certified Bob Ross painting instructor.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.920
Ross’ second wife Jane, was a civilian in
Alaska.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.620
They had one son, Morgan, who would also become
an accomplished artist.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.150
Sadly, Jane died from cancer in 1993 and Ross
did not remarry.

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.599
Military Career

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:20.709
Long before his time on public television,
Ross was in the military.

00:03:20.709 --> 00:03:25.420
At the age of 18 Ross joined the United States
Air Force and within a few years he was uprooted

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:30.980
from his native Florida and sent to an entirely
unfamiliar place -- Fairbanks, Alaska.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.310
Ross spent the next twenty years of his life
in Alaska -- working, raising a family, and

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:40.879
developing a love for painting amid the majestic
mountains, towering pines, and crystal clear

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:41.879
waters.

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:47.290
Examining Ross’ paintings over his lifetime,
one can see without question, the trees, peaks,

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:50.950
valleys and streams are distinctly Alaskan.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:55.049
Eventually Ross rose through the Air Force
ranks to Master Sergeant after holding positions

00:03:55.049 --> 00:04:00.090
as a medical records technician and first
sergeant of the Clinic at Eielson Air Force

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.090
Base.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.859
These positions required him to be, in his
own words, “tough” and “mean.”

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:09.760
He was "the guy who makes you scrub the latrine,
the guy who makes you make your bed, the guy

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.450
who screams at you for being late to work."

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:16.350
Ross gained a reputation for being hard on
his subordinates who nicknamed him “Bust

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.580
’em up Bobby.”

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:22.890
For anyone who has watched Ross on The Joy
of Painting, the image of him as the authoritative,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:26.220
military figure couldn’t be further from
his television personality.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.130
Apparently, Ross yelled so much during his
military career he never wanted to raise his

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.130
voice again after he left the Air Force.

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:36.500
Bob Ross the Painter

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:41.730
Ross’ first brush with painting occurred
at an art class at the Anchorage U.S.O.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.340
club but he clashed with his abstract-loving
instructors.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:49.160
According to Ross, they failed to teach him
the practical techniques of how to paint.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:53.430
“They’d tell you what makes a tree, but
they wouldn’t tell you how to paint a tree.”

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:57.440
Fortunately, the experience didn’t turn
him away from painting altogether.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.190
Ross soon found a more suitable painting instructor
on public television.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:05.450
Ross’ predecessor was an older, German painter
named Bill Alexander.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:10.940
He hosted a half-hour instructional painting
show, The Magic of Oil Painting, on PBS.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.960
Watching the show, Ross learned how to paint
in the alla prima style (Italian for “first

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.390
attempt”), also known in the art world as
“wet-on-wet.”

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:23.530
Ross perfected the technique -- which allowed
him to complete a finished work in a single

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:24.530
session.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:28.810
Unlike other painting methods, when an artist
uses alla prima, he or she can add wet paint

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:32.530
in layers without waiting for the paint to
fully dry.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.750
Alexander falsely claimed to have invented
the style but it actually dates back to the

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:39.280
fifteenth century in Flanders (modern-day
Belgium).

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.340
Alla Prima was popular with many famous artists
throughout history but enjoyed great fanfare

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.220
among the Impressionist painters in the nineteenth
century.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.520
Notably, it was a favorite technique of artists
Claude Monet and John Singer Sargent.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:56.460
After further honing his skills, Ross decided
to supplement his Air Force pay by selling

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:00.130
landscapes painted on the inside of gold prospecting
pans to tourists.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:04.960
It was a lucrative endeavor and when his earnings
from paintings surpassed his military salary,

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.900
he resigned to become a full-time artist.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.010
The Joy of Painting

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:16.460
Upon leaving the Air Force, Ross returned
to Florida and sought out Bill Alexander to

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.850
perfect his painting skills.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:21.930
By that time, Alexander taught art classes
in his spare time and happily took the eager

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:23.030
Ross under his wing.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:26.770
Alexander didn’t realize Ross would later
take everything he knew from these lessons,

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:32.590
make his own show, and rise to the level of
superstardom -- which eventually led to their

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:33.720
falling out.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.370
During their early meeting, Alexander was
impressed with Ross and he hired him as a

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.050
traveling tutor for the Alexander Magic Art
Supplies Company.

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:45.620
At the same time, nine hundred miles away
from Alexander’s studio in Clearwater, Florida,

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:51.210
a woman named Annette Kowalski was experiencing
a debilitating depression after losing her

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.490
oldest son in a traffic accident.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:58.030
Unable to get up, she laid in bed all day
watching television episodes of The Magic

00:06:58.030 --> 00:06:59.180
of Oil Painting.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:03.700
Desperate and hoping to cheer his wife up,
Walter Kowalski signed Annette up for a five-day

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:07.930
painting class with Alexander...or so she
thought.

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:11.570
Before Annette’s arrival, Alexander had
passed on his classes to an unknown instructor

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:13.120
known simply as Bob.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.480
Annette was incredibly disappointed when she
heard Alexander was not longer instructing,

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:21.710
but Walter insisted she go anyway saying,
“Get in the Car.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:22.879
We’re going.”

00:07:22.879 --> 00:07:27.560
Over the course of the five day painting class
whatever misgivings Annette first had about

00:07:27.560 --> 00:07:29.460
Ross completely disappeared.

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.130
In fact, taking that class, she recognized
there was something very special about him.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.690
Looking back years later Annette said, "I
was so mesmerized by Bob.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:43.720
"Somehow, he lifted me up out of that depression….I
just think that Bob knew how to woo people.”

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:46.310
Wooing people was Ross’ calling card.

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:50.820
Annette was on to something and saw in Ross
an opportunity to “put it in a bottle and

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:51.820
sell it.”

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:56.700
She believed Ross was capable of making a
fortune and she wanted in.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:08:00.690
After the class, Annette and her husband took
Ross out to dinner and brokered a business

00:08:00.690 --> 00:08:01.690
deal.

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:05.110
From that point on, Annette became Ross’
manager -- sinking her entire life savings

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:06.460
into the partnership.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:09.080
Ross and his wife ponied up a similar amount
of capital.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:13.950
As manager, Annette focused on taking out
ads in the local papers and setting up painting

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:15.700
seminars at various malls.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:19.180
She even established a toll-free telephone
hotline, 1-800-BOB-ROSS.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:24.210
But for people to really connect and experience
the allure of Bob Ross, she needed to get

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:26.550
him in front of as many people as possible.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:31.930
They filmed a commercial, and possibly a pilot
television show to sell to PBS executives.

00:08:31.930 --> 00:08:36.760
Eventually, all the hard work and hustle paid
off and Ross landed his own show on the network,

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:37.829
The Joy of Painting.

00:08:37.829 --> 00:08:41.599
The first episode aired on January 11, 1983.

00:08:41.599 --> 00:08:49.089
The Joy of Painting enjoyed an eleven year
run on PBS from 1983 to 1994, filming 403

00:08:49.089 --> 00:08:50.339
episodes in total.

00:08:50.339 --> 00:08:54.670
During the 30 minutes of airtime for each
show, Ross stood in a dark room with a blank

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:57.699
canvas and painted an imaginary landscape
scene.

00:08:57.699 --> 00:09:01.980
There were two cameras for the program, shot
in real-time -- a medium shot of Ross and

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:05.810
his canvas, and a close-up shot of the canvas
or palette.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:10.129
Ross faithfully instructed oil painting to
viewers at home in the alla prima style -- the

00:09:10.129 --> 00:09:13.120
same technique he previously learned from
Bill Alexander.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:17.750
With little more than smudges, blotches, and
scrapes from his palette knife, Ross created

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:18.750
masterpieces.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:23.040
To keep the lessons inexpensive for people,
the tools and color selection was limited.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:26.210
Though he surely had the technique down, Ross’
real

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:31.629
draw for audiences was his calming presence,
slower-paced speech, warm voice, and words

00:09:31.629 --> 00:09:34.310
of encouragement sprinkled in while instructing.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:38.319
In this way, Ross is often compared to Fred
Rogers, the iconic children’s show host

00:09:38.319 --> 00:09:39.959
of Mister Rogers Neighborhood.

00:09:39.959 --> 00:09:42.120
On The Joy of Painting, there was no pressure
from Ross.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.410
He believed everyone could “paint almighty
pictures” and “there are no mistakes,

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:47.850
only happy accidents.”

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:51.350
The Joy of Painting proved to be an immediate
success for the PBS network.

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:56.220
For some viewers, it was a reprieve from the
harsher realities of life, or the doldrums

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:57.949
of other television programs.

00:09:57.949 --> 00:10:02.230
When asked about his consoling approach, Ross
said, "I got a letter from somebody here a

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:05.860
while back, and they said, 'Bob, everything
in your world seems to be happy.'

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:06.899
That's for sure.

00:10:06.899 --> 00:10:07.899
That's why I paint.

00:10:07.899 --> 00:10:12.120
It's because I can create the kind of world
that I want, and I can make this world as

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.579
happy as I want it.

00:10:13.579 --> 00:10:16.790
Shoot, if you want bad stuff, watch the news.”

00:10:16.790 --> 00:10:21.579
The Joy of Painting was like a personal art
lesson, described by his manager Annette as

00:10:21.579 --> 00:10:22.949
“liquid tranquilizer.”

00:10:22.949 --> 00:10:26.899
Yet amazingly, only about 10 percent of viewers
painted along with Ross.

00:10:26.899 --> 00:10:29.740
The other 90 percent tuned in to simply watch
him.

00:10:29.740 --> 00:10:33.990
Ross worked quickly to create his masterpieces
-- swirling the paint around, dabbing it here

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:38.519
and there -- and grabbing for his two-inch
brush every now and then saying, “Now, let’s

00:10:38.519 --> 00:10:39.519
get crazy.”

00:10:39.519 --> 00:10:42.829
He made the whole process of painting look
completely effortless.

00:10:42.829 --> 00:10:48.319
The truth is, Ross was meticulous and his
manager Annette referred to him as a “tyrant.”

00:10:48.319 --> 00:10:53.499
Ross was not mean or nasty according to Annette
but he was every bit a businessman as he was

00:10:53.499 --> 00:10:54.499
a painter.

00:10:54.499 --> 00:10:58.730
He wanted everything on the show to be perfect
and done his way.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:02.889
Before filming each episode, Ross would lay
awake at night planning and rehearsing exactly

00:11:02.889 --> 00:11:03.970
what he would say.

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:08.749
No one watching would ever know -- Ross always
appeared to be calm and free form on camera.

00:11:08.749 --> 00:11:11.190
His planning extended to more than the words
he said.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:15.379
For each episode of The Joy of Painting, Ross
didn’t complete just one painting.

00:11:15.379 --> 00:11:17.250
He always finished three.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:21.459
Ross painted one while filming (the one everyone
saw), another was done before and sat just

00:11:21.459 --> 00:11:26.000
off-camera for Ross to use as reference, and
the final painting, a more detailed work,

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.790
was completed after the show and was photographed
for use in Ross’ instruction books.

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.510
Ross was never actually paid for appearing
in any of The Joys of Painting shows on PBS.

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:37.980
Instead, he used it to promote his teaching
business -- which worked spectacularly to

00:11:37.980 --> 00:11:41.100
expand interest after the first episode.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:45.290
The business, Bob Ross Inc., eventually grew
to include paint supplies branded by Bob Ross

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.959
and the company and Ross raked in millions
of dollars.

00:11:47.959 --> 00:11:51.769
To further bring his brand into the spotlight,
Ross occasionally appeared on talk shows and

00:11:51.769 --> 00:11:56.399
appeared in promotional gigs; some of these
included MTV, The Joan Rivers Show, Phil Donahue,

00:11:56.399 --> 00:11:59.412
and as a guest at a Hank Snow country music
concert at the Grand Ole Opry.

00:11:59.412 --> 00:12:05.089
The final The Joy of Painting show aired on
May 17, 1994 after Ross was diagnosed with

00:12:05.089 --> 00:12:06.089
cancer.

00:12:06.089 --> 00:12:08.899
Ross’ son Steven, who made regular appearances
on the show, appeared with his father on the

00:12:08.899 --> 00:12:10.069
set.

00:12:10.069 --> 00:12:14.779
Bob Ross the Brand

00:12:14.779 --> 00:12:18.579
Most of Ross’ image was intentionally created
-- except for his trademark afro.

00:12:18.579 --> 00:12:22.550
Believe it or not, his hair wasn’t even
naturally curly -- it was straight.

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:26.290
Before he officially had ‘made it,’ Ross
ditched his short, military crew cuts for

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:28.930
a longer style and perm as a way to save money.

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:32.980
Once his perm was a hallmark of the Bob Ross
brand, he couldn’t bring himself to change

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.269
it even though he grew to dislike it.

00:12:35.269 --> 00:12:39.560
Later, after Ross got sick from Lymphoma cancer
and lost all his hair to chemotherapy treatments,

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:42.330
he wore a wig on his show to keep up his appearance.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.629
Ross’ outfits were another carefully crafted
element of his “look.”

00:12:45.629 --> 00:12:49.899
On The Joy of Painting Ross wore casual blue
jeans with a button-up shirt...the top buttons

00:12:49.899 --> 00:12:52.490
were left undone to reveal a bit of chest
hair.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:56.499
This timeless look was intended to appear
“current” when PBS aired reruns of shows

00:12:56.499 --> 00:12:57.499
later on.

00:12:57.499 --> 00:13:01.129
A private man, Ross and his manager Annette
told a backstory of his life that was light

00:13:01.129 --> 00:13:02.839
on biographical details.

00:13:02.839 --> 00:13:06.790
The story emphasized modest beginnings, a
love of nature and animals, and philosophy

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:08.399
that every person matters.

00:13:08.399 --> 00:13:13.249
His caring personality extended to his viewers,
students, and injured animals that he took

00:13:13.249 --> 00:13:14.339
in and cared for.

00:13:14.339 --> 00:13:18.030
The narrative continues to be pushed today
by the business, Bob Ross, Inc.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:20.730
Illness &amp; Death

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:27.660
In 1994, Ross was dealt a devastating blow
with his diagnosis of Lymphoma, a rare type

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:28.660
of cancer.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:32.180
Lymphoma is in a group of blood cancers -- it
attacks the lymphatic system.

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:35.970
The lymph nodes and glands, along with a network
of vessels, are an important part of the body’s

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:39.390
circulatory functions that aid in a person’s
immune system.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:43.360
Many people with Lymphoma never have or display
symptoms, and it isn’t unknown how much

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.459
Ross suffered from his disease.

00:13:45.459 --> 00:13:49.579
Ross kept his cancer diagnosis out of the
public eye, only telling his closest friends

00:13:49.579 --> 00:13:50.579
and relatives.

00:13:50.579 --> 00:13:54.869
He reportedly painted right up until the end,
and died in 1995 at the age of 52.

00:13:54.869 --> 00:13:59.369
At his grave at Woodlawn Memorial Park in
Gotha, Florida, a simple stone marker reads:

00:13:59.369 --> 00:14:05.839
“Bob Ross, Television Artist.”

00:14:05.839 --> 00:14:06.839
Bob Ross’ Legacy

00:14:06.839 --> 00:14:10.920
Bob Ross was a prolific artist, purportedly
completing 30,000 paintings during his lifetime.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:15.269
With so many Ross paintings out in the world,
it would seem relatively easy to find one

00:14:15.269 --> 00:14:18.670
and buy it to hang above the living room couch.

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:23.459
Not true -- anyone wanting to purchase one
today would be hard-pressed to find an original.

00:14:23.459 --> 00:14:27.330
The reason for this being, there are so many
copycat and fraudulent versions that have

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:28.600
flooded the marketplace.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:33.920
And, virtually all of Ross’ paintings made
on The Joy of Painting he donated to PBS fundraisers

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:37.189
to raise money for other public broadcasting
programs.

00:14:37.189 --> 00:14:41.009
Today the company, Bob Ross Inc. is going
strong and is still managed by Annette and

00:14:41.009 --> 00:14:42.189
Walter Kowalski.

00:14:42.189 --> 00:14:46.800
The company sells a variety of paints, brushes,
art kits, easels, canvases, instructional

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:47.910
DVDs and books.

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:53.009
On their website one can find and order Bob
Ross branded apparel -- including socks, T-shirts,

00:14:53.009 --> 00:14:54.129
and baby bibs.

00:14:54.129 --> 00:14:59.199
There are an array of accessories and wacky
gifts, including coffee mugs, glasses -- even

00:14:59.199 --> 00:15:01.769
a certified Bob Ross Chia Pet.

00:15:01.769 --> 00:15:05.629
Beyond the official merchandise, one can find
just about anything with the Bob Ross image

00:15:05.629 --> 00:15:09.740
-- a quick Google search returns a prayer
candle named for ‘Saint Bob Ross.’

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:13.810
The company Bob Ross, Inc. still trains and
certifies painting instructors who desire

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:17.220
to work, teach, and promote themselves in
the spirit of the artist.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:21.869
Most recently, Netflix has licensed shows,
adding Ross’ series, Beauty Is Everywhere

00:15:21.869 --> 00:15:24.100
and The Joy of Painting to its lineup.

00:15:24.100 --> 00:15:28.000
In pop-culture, references to Bob Ross and
his aphorisms are everywhere.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:31.639
His likeness and inspirational quotes live
on through internet memes.

00:15:31.639 --> 00:15:35.830
Google celebrated Ross for what would have
been his seventieth birthday in 2012 with

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:40.100
an Google Doodle, an image of Ross painting
the letter “g” with a landscape in the

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:41.100
background.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:44.730
He has been mentioned in many popular television
shows such as Family Guy and The Boondocks;

00:15:44.730 --> 00:15:49.509
Target stores carry a Bob Ross board game,
Bob Ross: The Art of Chill.

00:15:49.509 --> 00:15:54.100
If all this wasn’t enough, the LEGO company
has created a Bob Ross LEGO figure, complete

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:55.839
with a paintbrush and palette.

00:15:55.839 --> 00:16:00.189
His icon status shows absolutely no signs
of diminishing.

00:16:00.189 --> 00:16:04.870
It seems Ross’ legacy will never die -- and
his fans will continue to honor and celebrate

00:16:04.870 --> 00:16:06.490
the man for years to come.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:09.259
Ross once said, “Didn’t you know you had
that much power?

00:16:09.259 --> 00:16:11.050
You can move mountains.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:12.339
You can do anything.”

00:16:12.339 --> 00:16:16.860
He showed us he could create a better place
-- a world filled with “happy little trees”

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:21.629
that was free of judgement -- where everyone
was an artist and every landscape was full

00:16:21.629 --> 00:16:55.689
of majestic wonder.

